3:Revision 2, 2026-11-16, after D34 (changes requested). What changed from revision 1: cash deposits count as trading receipts and the large ones are flagged (D31); the pass/refer/fail outcome is shown with an illustrative label (D28); the tool does not time the underwriter (D32); and the pack is written once, at sign-off. Everything else stands as revision 1 designed it, and the owner confirmed local-first (D29), OBIE v3.1 and TrueLayer first (D30) and Tier 1 only by 2026-11-29 (D33).
39:**Option A: local review workspace (chosen).** The cma package serves a web page on the underwriter's machine, reachable only from that machine (loopback). Every figure, bucket and monthly cell opens its lines, and the lines needing judgement come first. The underwriter re-categorises lines with a reason and the figures layer recomputes, along with the indicators and the illustrative policy check. A named sign-off writes a memo-ready pack and a self-contained HTML record. The model is off by default.
41:**Option B: static HTML report plus a CLI overrides file.** Each run writes one self-contained HTML file with click-through. Corrections are typed into an overrides file and the run repeated; sign-off stays at the terminal. This is the cheapest option and needs no server. *It lost because* the two things the underwriter asked for beyond click-through, correcting categories and a usable summary, would happen in a file-editing loop outside the page, and the underwriter would see the effect of a correction on the figures and the policy check only after a re-run. It is kept as the format of the signed record (the pack's HTML).
55:**Sub-decision: how sign-off works.** (i) Drive the existing LangGraph review interrupt from the workspace. (ii) Make the case folder the source of truth, and make sign-off a case-log event bound to a snapshot hash. *Chose (ii)*. The figures are a pure function of the case's inputs, so a checkpoint would hold nothing extra, and re-running the graph after every override would also re-run the draft node. ReviewDecision's name and notes validation is reused, not copied. `cma run` keeps its interrupt unchanged. Revision 2 settles the order: the underwriter signs a preview, and the pack is written once, at sign-off, from that preview. Revision 1 said both that sign-off binds to a pack already built and that a pack is never overwritten, which left nowhere to record the sign-off.
78:          +--> preview (field map, section text, guard) --> sign-off (case log event)
87:- **C6 Preview, pack and sign-off** (new src/cma/pack.py). Memo field map, deterministic section text, the labelled policy-check section, grounding guard, HTML record, evidence JSON v1, monthly CSV, sign-off binding to the preview's snapshot, and an atomic write of the pack once, at sign-off.
142:Tier 1, demonstrating the mechanism (D23): R1-R7, R10-R19, R22-R31, R33-R35, R42, R43, R47, R48, R52-R56 (39 requirements), plus the stopwatch trial sheet (documentation only). It covers the v2 tables with real acquirers, lenders and cash wording, the buckets with cash inside trading receipts, the monthly summary and indicators, single-account cases with a profile and an override log, the workspace with the illustrative policy check, the preview, pack and sign-off, the offline lock, and a synthetic case shaped like a business.
167:- **Security.** *Who can call it:* the operating-system user who owns the case root. The workspace also needs a per-launch token and a loopback Host header (R33). *Trust boundary:* imported files and payer text are checked by the loader and escaped on output (R34). *Secrets:* only an optional model key in the environment, never logged. *Personal data:* kept under one case root readable only by its owner, never committed to git (R31), never in prompts (R30); evidence holds only hashes of the profile. *Risk path:* lending data; the human acknowledgement is the named sign-off bound to a snapshot that includes the policy hash and whether the check was shown (R22). The illustrative outcome is not an acknowledgement. *Worst case:* see Security notes, now including cash paid in to look like trading.
170:- **Clean code.** *What changes most:* the rule lists and thresholds, kept apart as data. *Names:* follow the code: money in (inflows in metrics.py), trading receipts (M2 keeps its name, Total revenue, so signed memos still read), override, firm rule, case, preview, pack, sign-off, policy check. *Tests:* properties P1-P20, synthetic truth for every bucket and indicator, and golden files for the importers. *Duplication removed:* all importers share one balance walk; sign-off reuses ReviewDecision's validation; the pack's policy section reuses policy.evaluate and the guard's [Rn] check. *Duplication introduced:* the v1 rule table, kept as data for one release. *No single-caller abstraction:* no storage interface for a future hosted version; the label is one constant, not a templating layer; the importer contract has four callers.
171:- **Data-intensive.** *Idempotency:* imports are keyed by file hash, an identical override changes nothing, and a second sign-off is refused. *Ordering and concurrency:* one writer per case, enforced by a lock plus the expected log position; a second tab gets a conflict and reloads. *Partial failure:* append-only logs with a check for a torn last entry; the pack is written to a temporary folder and then renamed. *Consistency:* the workspace reads the latest state; the preview and sign-off bind to a snapshot that includes thresholds, the policy hash and the show_policy_verdict setting, so a sign-off cannot attach to an outcome the underwriter did not see. *Migration:* additive, nothing to backfill. *Money:* integer pence, GBP only, thresholds compared in pence, and amounts with more than two decimals are refused.
234:      packs/<snapshot>/  written once at sign-off: memo.md, record.html, evidence.json, monthly.csv
238:**Evidence JSON v1.** Everything in today's record, with its policy key unchanged. Added: schema_version, case id, accounts (key, source format, file hash, period, line count), ruleset id and hash, firm rules hash, thresholds hash, profile hash, overrides (sequence number, line, old and new category, reason, name, OS user, time), money-in buckets, monthly summary, indicators with the thresholds that fired, policy_check (shown or hidden, the label, the policy name and hash), snapshot hash, sign-off (name, OS user, time, notes), egress (offline, or the model's name) and package version.
250:- `cma case sign CASE` asks for a typed name, checks the snapshot against the preview and writes the pack once. It is interactive only; there is no --yes. Preview and sign-off are also available in the workspace.
262:**Pack.** memo.md has the sections in field-map order, figures with their ids, and blanks left for the underwriter. While the check is shown, it then has a 'Policy check (illustrative)' section that no memo field maps to. Its first line is the label, followed by the overall outcome and each rule, with the metric value tagged [Mn] and the threshold tagged [Rn] after a limit word. record.html is self-contained and makes no external requests; it shows the label wherever an outcome appears, and shows the sign-off. The pack also holds evidence.json (v1) and monthly.csv. It is written once, at sign-off, to a new folder named by the snapshot hash.
289:| An override changes the illustrative outcome | The panel recomputes; a sign-off on a preview that showed the old outcome is refused as stale | R25, R23 |
295:| Power is lost while a log entry is being written | The torn last entry is detected; sign-off is refused until the underwriter confirms dropping it | R43 |
296:| The tool crashes while writing the pack at sign-off | The pack goes to a temporary folder and is renamed; leftovers are removed on the next start; no partial pack is visible, and the sign-off entry is written only after the rename | R26 |
300:| Someone edits the case log by hand | The chain check fails; sign-off is refused and the first bad entry named | R43 |
332:3. Land the case store, the workspace with the illustrative policy check, preview, pack and sign-off, and the offline lock. Demo on the owner's statements and on a synthetic case shaped like a business, both marked as demos (D23).
357:- **Risk path:** lending data (risk: data). The human acknowledgement is the named sign-off bound to the snapshot. The name is typed, not authenticated (README known limit), so the OS user is recorded alongside it. Real sign-in belongs to a hosted version. The illustrative outcome is computed, labelled and never signed as a decision.
